// The vulkan package: raw bindings to Vulkan 1.3, written by hand from the
// specification (registry.khronos.org/vulkan), the part of it that clearing a
// window through a swapchain needs, and the part that drawing through a
// pipeline needs (shaders, pipelines, descriptors, images, buffers), and
// computing and timing on the GPU (compute pipelines, dispatch, indirect
// dispatch and draws, timestamp queries).
//
// Names are Vulkan's without its prefix, in Cone's style:
//
// - A function drops 'vk' and starts lower case: vkCreateInstance is
//   'vulkan.createInstance', vkCmdBeginRendering is 'vulkan.cmdBeginRendering'.
//   Its parameters are the specification's, names and order.
// - A type drops 'Vk': VkInstanceCreateInfo is 'vulkan.InstanceCreateInfo',
//   its fields named and ordered as the specification has them.
// - A constant drops 'VK_': VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO is
//   'vulkan.STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO', VK_FORMAT_B8G8R8A8_SRGB is
//   'vulkan.FORMAT_B8G8R8A8_SRGB'. Enumerations and flag bits are all
//   constants of their C width (u32; VkFlags64 is u64; VkResult is i32), since
//   OR-ing flags does not make a variant, and a driver may return a value a
//   typed enum would not list.
// - A function-pointer type drops 'PFN_vk': 'vulkan.PFN_CreateInstance'.
//
// Handles are as the specification defines them. A dispatchable handle
// (Instance, PhysicalDevice, Device, Queue, CommandBuffer) is a pointer to an
// opaque struct, '0usize as vulkan.Instance' being VK_NULL_HANDLE. A
// non-dispatchable one (Image, Buffer, Fence, ...) is a struct holding the
// u64, so that each is its own type: 'new vulkan.Buffer(0u64)' is its null.
//
// Every struct is laid out as C lays it out: fields in the specification's
// order, not reordered, not packed. The package's 'layout' test checks each
// size and offset against the C compiler. A struct with an 'sType' gives it
// its own value by default, its 'pNext' defaults to null, and every count and
// flags field defaults to 0, so a create-info names only what it sets:
//
//   imm ci = new vulkan.FenceCreateInfo(flags: vulkan.FENCE_CREATE_SIGNALED_BIT);
//
// Every other pointer field has no default, so each is written, a null one as
// 'null'.
//
// Nothing is linked. Every function is found at run time, as the volk loader
// does it, in three steps, each after what it needs exists, and each step's
// functions belong to the object they were found for, at Vulkan's own levels:
//
//   the module's 'init'            opens the OS's Vulkan loader (vulkan-1.dll,
//                                  libvulkan.so.1) and finds vkGetInstanceProcAddr
//                                  and the functions needing no instance,
//                                  once, before main: the global-level
//                                  functions, called as 'vulkan.createInstance'
//                                  and the like. With no Vulkan driver it
//                                  does not fail: 'vulkan.available()' is
//                                  false
//   InstanceFns.load(instance)     the instance's functions, vkGetDeviceProcAddr
//                                  among them, a table for that instance:
//                                  'fns.destroyInstance(instance, null)'
//   DeviceFns.load(&fns, device)   the device's functions, found through the
//                                  instance's vkGetDeviceProcAddr, so they
//                                  call the driver directly, not through the
//                                  loader's dispatch, a table for that
//                                  device: 'dfns.cmdDraw(commandBuffer, ...)'
//
// Each table's 'missing' names how many of the functions it looks for were
// not found; a function of an extension that was not enabled (debug utils) is
// optional, and not counted. Several instances and devices are legal, each
// with its own table. Calling a global-level function while 'available' is
// false, or a table's function that was missing, calls address 0 and crashes.
// 'GlobalFns.find' and 'InstanceFns.loadWith' take any vkGetInstanceProcAddr,
// which is how the package's loader test stands one in.
//
// Not followed: SDL's SDL_VULKAN_LIBRARY hint. 'init' opens the OS's default
// loader, while SDL, which loads its own copy for surfaces, would honour the
// hint, so a program that sets it gets two different loaders.
//
// The addresses are called as Cone function references, which use C's calling
// convention. VKAPI_CALL is empty on 64-bit Windows, so the two agree; a
// 32-bit build would need the system convention, which Cone does not have yet.
//
// Everything is as C has it: C types, C strings ('*u8', zero-terminated, as a
// string literal is), and structs through pointers. Vulkan passes no struct
// by value, which matters because a call through a function reference passes
// a struct by value as Cone does, not as C does (only a C-named function's
// calls follow C's convention for structs).
//
// Its files are one module:
//
//   handles.cone          versions, handles, and VkResult's values and names
//   constants.cone        the constants: enumerations and flag bits
//   structs.cone          structs: shared small ones, the instance, physical
//                         devices, the device and queues, surfaces and
//                         swapchains, images
//   commandstructs.cone   structs: commands, synchronization, submission,
//                         buffers and memory
//   pipelinestructs.cone  structs: shaders, pipelines, descriptors
//   fntypes.cone          the functions, as function-reference types
//   load.cone             the OS's loader, the module's 'init', and the
//                         global-level functions (GlobalFns)
//   instancefns.cone      the instance-level functions (InstanceFns)
//   devicefns.cone        the device-level functions (DeviceFns)
//
// The layout test's tests/layout.py reads them all, and lists them.

mod vulkan;
